    "q-138085": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138085",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
